<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">United States Senate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as U.S. Senate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Senate is the upper house of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-congress">United States Congress</a>, established by the constitution of 1787 and first convened in 1789, with two senators from each state. Its approval by a two-thirds majority is required to ratify treaties, giving it a decisive voice over agreements such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hay-bunau-varilla-treaty">Hay–Bunau-Varilla Treaty</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torrijos-carter-treaties">Torrijos–Carter Treaties</a>. In 1902 it voted for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> route over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> for an interoceanic canal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-states-senate.jpg" alt="United States Senate" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis Dreka designed the actual seal, first used in 1885 per here. Vectorized from a version in stained glass.; Original:; Maksim; Vector … · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_the_United_States_Senate.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The drafters of the Constitution debated how to award representation in the Senate more than any other part of the document. Bicameralism and a proportional people's house were widely popular, but Senate representation proved contentious. Some small states, unwilling to give up the equal power they held under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/articles-of-confederation">Articles of Confederation</a>, threatened to secede in 1787, and they won by a vote of 5 to 4 in what became known as the Connecticut Compromise. It provided that each state, whatever its population, would have two senators. The Senate's composition and powers are set out in Article One of the Constitution, which has been in effect since 4 March 1789.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1789 until 1913 senators were appointed by the legislatures of their states. By the early 20th century the legislatures of as many as 29 states had provided for popular election of senators by referendum, and in 1913 the ratification of the Seventeenth Amendment made election by statewide popular vote the national rule. The Senate has expelled fifteen senators in its history: William Blount for treason in 1797, and fourteen in 1861 and 1862 for supporting Confederate secession. In 1837 it chose the vice president, Richard Mentor Johnson, after a deadlock in the Electoral College, the only time it has done so.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Senate has two senators from each state who serve staggered six-year terms. A senator must be at least 30 years old, a citizen for at least nine years and an inhabitant of the state represented. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> argued in Federalist No. 62 that this called for a greater extent of information and stability of character. The vice president is president of the Senate and votes only to break a tie, and in the vice president's absence the president pro tempore presides. Much of the work is done in committees, and a characteristic practice is the filibuster and its remedy, the vote on cloture.</p><p>The Senate must consent to treaties by a two-thirds vote of the senators present, confirms judges, ambassadors, Cabinet members and many other officials, and tries impeachments brought by the House of Representatives, where conviction needs a two-thirds majority. Bills for raising revenue must originate in the House, a rule based on the practice of the Parliament of the United Kingdom. Critics such as the political scientist Robert Dahl have called its equal representation of states a profound violation of political equality among citizens, while defenders argue that it gives stability and protects the voice of smaller states.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “United States Senate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The prospect of a Nicaraguan canal shaped British and American rivalry in the Caribbean, producing the Clayton–Bulwer Treaty of 1850, and the route remained the main alternative to Panama until the <b>United States Senate</b> chose Panama in 1902.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Torrijos–Carter Treaties of 1977, approved by Panamanian voters in a plebiscite and narrowly ratified by the <b>United States Senate</b> in 1978, the Canal Zone was abolished on 1 October 1979, a binational commission ran the canal for two decades, and a separate Neutrality Treaty guaranteed its…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Báez's six-year rule of 1868 to 1874 negotiated annexation to the United States, which President Grant supported but the <b>US Senate</b> rejected in 1870.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-dominican-republic">Second Dominican Republic</a></li></ul></section>
